ef 17592186163152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162165 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162165 #edb/ref 1045 "The Joint CDF of Two Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162165 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186163101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162165 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162165 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162165 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5a4d4eca-048a-559f-8986-fcbff586afd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162415 #edb/ref 1069 "q-145626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162415 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y)$, where\n\n$F(9, 10) = 0.65, F(6, 10) = 0.31, F(3, 10) = 0.12$,\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$S_{X} = {3, 6, 9, 12}, S_{Y} = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12}$.\n\nFind $P(3 < X \\le 9, Y \\le 10)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162415 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56a68cea-c574-533e-9e2c-ea532c1a718c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162415 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162421 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162421 #edb/ref 1069 "q-145909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162421 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y)$, where\n\n$F(7, 1) = \\frac{9}{16}, F(7,-2) = \\frac{1}{4}, F(3, 1) = \\frac{1}{8}, F(3,-2) = \\frac{1}{16}$,\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$S_{X} = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}, S_{Y} = {-2,-1, 0, 1, 2}$.\n\nFind $P(5 \\le X \\le 8,-1 \\le Y \\le 1)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162421 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4909f9a0-a8a3-5a67-aa19-15cc754b3e1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162421 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163101 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163101 #edb/ref 1061 38838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163101 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "494e3329-5465-5724-b614-4ecacfaaed97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163101 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163102 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163102 #edb/ref 1061 38839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163102 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f666bcd7-dca8-55b3-aedf-a8ef91edbe96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163102 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163103 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163103 #edb/ref 1061 38840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163103 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59dbcbcc-3e03-5e5e-946c-753994f1060e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163103 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163108 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163108 #edb/ref 1061 38938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163108 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ccba458-0f60-53d1-9e72-3de4cf2a6b1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163108 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163109 #edb/ref 1061 38939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163109 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2851f1ef-e083-54cd-a8c0-7dd89a1c3344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163109 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163152 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163152 #edb/ref 1061 42192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163152 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d0f7f20-6219-5e6b-9d38-ad689f056506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163152 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163341 #edb/ref 1064 "The **joint cumulative distribution function** (or joint CDF) of two discrete random variables $X,$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P( X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)  = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nThe sets $S_X$ and $S_Y,$ on which the variables $X$ and $Y$ are defined, are the **supports** of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWe can see from this definition that the joint CDF is analogous to the cumulative distribution function of a single random variable.\n\nConsider the joint PMF for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nHere, the supports of $X$ and $Y$ are $S_X = \\{ 0, 1 \\}$ and $S_Y = \\{ 0, 1, 2 \\},$ respectively. \n\nAs an example, let's compute $F(1,1).$ By definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X \\leq 1, Y \\leq 1.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =f(0,0)+f(0,1)+f(1,0)+f(1,1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.3+0.2 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163341 #edb/ref 1065 6430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163341 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "68282cd4-eb49-54e1-a97a-8d49988c8922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163341 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163360 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the joint PMF for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWe can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\neq 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n![](images/c6/c6d8e4012a0a16f865bf229549512ff772e103d3fcd7667738cf7250d7267e84.png)\n\n \n\nNow, the joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nGeometrically, then, the joint CDF gives the total \"probability mass\" that lies inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nFor example, consider the point $(2,1.5)$ and the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at this point, shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46837da3e8f8f44831da4e6b464e9f8562f322b17f584df3fc87912d1c09d4c4.png)\n\nThe value of $F(2,1.5)$ equals the total \"probability mass\" contained within this rectangle. That is,\n\n$$\nF(2,1.5) = f(1,0) + f(2,0) + f(1,1) + f(2,1).\n$$\n\nIt's worth highlighting a few important cases:\n\n- If our rectangular region doesn't include any points where the probability is nonzero, then we must have $F(x,y) = 0.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb5faa2b42c9ece2fe80cff4dd27a8b720cb363f5319c3cb5f5dd065f818c403.png)\n\n- If our rectangular region includes *all* points where the probability is nonzero, then we must have $F(x,y) = 1.$\n\n![](images/81/811b15e3cea46e0a4a546e68217a2994a7b2a7b80d6bc444bc6fbaa2ebaed355.png)\n\nFinally, we note the following properties of the joint CDF:\n\n- $0 \\leq F(x,y) \\leq 1$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by $F_X(x) = F(x, \\infty).$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $Y$ is given by $F_Y(y) = F( \\infty, y).$\n\n- $F(\\infty, \\infty) = 1$\n\n- $F(-\\infty,y) = F(x, -\\infty) = 0$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then $F(x,y) = F_X(x) \\cdot F_Y(y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163360 #edb/ref 1065 6977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163360 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d6a285ac-6fdf-506c-9228-20857fedc5b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163360 #edb/ref 1067 "Geometric Interpretation of the Joint CDF" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
